let's bring in white house press  secretary jen saki she's here ahead of  president biden's first u.n general  assembly address as president and uh jen  i want to get to that uh it's a big  address from the president but these  images along the border are shocking and  the bar is high for shocking images  along the border you've called them  horrific  what did the president say how did he  feel when he saw them he also felt they  were horrific and horrible i don't know  anyone who could watch that video and  not have that emotion  last night our secretary of homeland  security put out a statement conveying  there would be an investigation that  he's going to send additional personnel  i think it's important for people to  know this is not who we are that's not  who the biden harris administration is  and we're going to absolutely pursue  that investigation and get to the bottom  of what happened here meanwhile  deportation flights are ongoing and i  think what's confusing for people there  is secretary my orcis back in may said  to haitian nationals already here we  know things are so terrible in your  country that you're going to get a  temporary extension we're not going to  send you back your case is going to be  ongoing you're going to be here  shouldn't that same idea apply now  you're right temporary protected status  which is something the secretary and the  secretary of state also put in place  they actually extended it further  because of the turmoil on the ground  into later in the summer now anyone who  is here before then may be eligible for  temporary protected status  and we're looking into that people may  be eligible for that but right now we  also have to implement our laws at the  border we also want to protect people  both in that community but also migrants  one of the challenges as we're all  facing a pandemic here is the gathering  of so many people we're still implanting  title 42 which means that we are going  to send people out of the country who  come in as we implement that a coveted  safety protocol exactly but did you say  that it's possible that that extension  that applies to haitians already here  could apply to those coming across the  border well tony it's already been  extended uh because of the turmoil on  the ground it was earlier this summer  that's something that the secretary of  homeland security and secretary of state  do look into but again as we look to the  the photos not just the ones you  referenced but but of all of these  families and people under the bridges we  wanted to also take steps to implement  our laws and to protect a lot of them  from the spread of covet as well you  know there's a lot of incoming jen  at the white house these days what will  be the president's message today at the  united general assembly well he he  appears to be really under the gun on so  many different levels well the way the  president views it and we all view it is  that's what you do as president you  navigate crises you weather storms and  we're certainly doing that right now but  this is a huge opportunity today for the  president he's obviously been on the  world stage for a long time but he's  going to say to the world all the  leaders in the room we're not looking  inward after we end a 20-year war we're  going to look outward and we're going to  prioritize what's most important to  address whether that's the climate  crisis whether that's cyber threats  continuing to work with partners on  counter terrorism  and that's what we need to have our  resources and focus and our eyes on and  we're going to do that through  rebuilding our allies and partnerships i  think it's good to say we're going to  look outward but we can't ignore it has  happened before we're still getting  hammered for  how the the withdrawal from afghanistan  happened everybody knows every many  people believe it was time it's just the  way that it was done so we all agree  with that that's not a good look you  look at what's happening with  immigration you look at france now  saying that  they've been betrayed by the united  states so i get that we have to look  forward but what are we doing to  justify or explain what appears to be  very bad behavior on our part now  we don't see it that way one we have a  long abiding relationship friendship  alliance with france what we're talking  about here this is about an economic  deal we have the best nuclear sub  technology in the world and the  australians wanted our technology prior  to that they were buying uh they were  buying uh this from france we understand  that they're they're just pleased about  that but we have a long abiding  friendship with them that's going to  endure and and we are going to rely on  that long history of friendships but i  think the president is the first to say  you've got to work on relationships we  know this in our real lives as well  and he's and he's here to do that too so  when he speaks to the world today he's  going to convey alliances are the  backbone of who we are we're going to  build and address these issues in the  world based on those alliances climate  cyber counterterrorism whatever it may  be now speaking of those relationships  how does he plan to address the  relationship with china that has been  described as dysfunctional  well i we heard the un secretary general  describe it that way we don't agree with  that assessment the president met with  him uh last night and conveyed uh you do  not have to be concerned that we're  trying to start a cold war with china  that is not what the united states is  going to do and the president will  address that in his speech today but we  do see the relationship through the  prism of competition it is a china is a  competitor but it's not a country we  want to have conflict with so what  people will hear the president talk  about is where we have issues we will  raise them but we will also look to  areas where we can work together and we  need to work together and the u.n  secretary general has also said that on  climate change time is up that needs to  unite the world action needs to happen  meanwhile here in this country joe  manchin senator from west virginia made  a half million dollars on coal  production last year  his son runs a coal company that he  founded and he's got more donations from  fossil fuel companies than anyone else  in the senate what's the world supposed  to think when they see that's the  individual helping shape climate policy  here in this country well i think a lot  of people in congress would argue that  he's start he's shaping climate policy  in the united states we do need his vote  in order to get the president's package  across the finish line that is going to  be a historic investment in addressing  the climate crisis president agrees with  the u.n secretary general but what we're  trying to do right now we're navigating  through sausage making is messy so even  as the president's at the u.n general  assembly our team at home is working  with members of congress to get this  package that has a huge climate  investment across the finish line the  world will certainly be watching you're  right and americans should feel  confident in that climate package given  the author of it or a key shaper of that  policy joe manchin well i think  americans can look at the details  american people are smart we don't need  to underestimate their intelligence out  there not suggesting you are not  suggesting you are but anyone who is  should not do that what they can see is  this is a historic investment in  electric vehicles and setting high  standards and making sure that we are  making the investment now to address  what is a crisis that is happening now  we need to just take steps now all right  well it's nothing yet but it's being  worked on we're getting there they will  stay with us  later  that's right very messy always all right  jen thank you very much thank you  appreciate it

